From f6f374a723c992315f36ceae0d378221d6640ef8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:34:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: a redirection's `&` is never a
 background operator (`cat r.sh 2>&1 | bash`)

The shell tokenizer (shlex with `&` as punctuation) split `2>&1` into
`2>`, `&`, `1`. The walk read that lone `&` as a background operator, so
`cat r.sh 2>&1 | bash` became `cat r.sh 2>` (output routed nowhere) and
`1 | bash`: the pipe fed `bash` nothing and scan_gateway_lifecycle
returned (False, None) for a script that restarts the gateway. Same for
`{ cat r.sh; } 2>&1 | bash`, `(cat r.sh) 2>&1 | bash`, `>&2`, `<&0`,
`2>&-`, `>&f`, `&>f`, `&>>f`, and for a reader carrying one
(`bash 2>&1 < r.sh`, `bash 2>&1 r.sh`).

The data-sink exemption tokenized the same way, so `env >& grep hermes
gateway restart` (env runs the words after the redirection target) was
masked as grep data and allowed.

Fix:
- split_glued_redirections quotes an unquoted `&` that belongs to a
  redirection operator (after an unquoted `<`/`>`, or `&>`/`&>>` not
  preceded by `&`/`|`), so shlex keeps it in the redirection word.
  `cmd & bash`, `&&` and `|&` stay control operators; a quoted or escaped
  `>` before `&` is text.
- _REDIRECTION also matches `&>` and `&>>`.
- _mask_data_sink_arguments tokenizes through split_glued_redirections.
- _split_redirections: `<&N` after `N<f` in the same command reads f
  (`bash 3<r.sh <&3`, `{ bash; } 3<r.sh <&3`, `cat 3<r.sh <&3 | bash`
  were also allowed).

Over-blocking note: the walk does not model stdout redirections, so
`cat r.sh &>/dev/null | bash` now blocks, as `cat r.sh >/dev/null | bash`
already did (fail closed).

RED/GREEN: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_descriptor_dup.py: 28 failed /
21 passed with cron/ at origin/main, 49 passed at head. tests/cron via
run_tests.sh: 238 files, 2974 passed, 0 failed, 9 skipped.

Card: t_01a1d655f3e69b6e (found in t_01a1d5c442ed5fc9).
